Bike Sharing Demand Prediction

Project Overview

This project predicts hourly bike rental demand using Linear Regression.

The dataset contains information about bike rentals along with temporal, weather, and environmental variables. The objective is to understand the factors associated with rental demand and build a regression model capable of predicting total bike rentals.

Dataset

The dataset contains hourly bike-sharing records with variables related to:

- Date and time
- Season
- Weather conditions
- Temperature
- Humidity
- Windspeed
- Working days and holidays
- Total bike rentals

Target Variable

- `cnt` — total number of bike rentals

Methodology

- Data exploration and quality checks
- Missing-value and duplicate checks
- Exploratory data analysis
- Correlation analysis
- Feature selection
- Feature encoding
- Feature scaling
- Target transformation using `log1p`
- Linear Regression
- Cross-validation
- Residual analysis
- Predicted vs actual analysis
- Model interpretation using regression coefficients

Feature Engineering

The variables `casual` and `registered` were excluded because they directly contribute to the target variable:

`cnt = casual + registered`

Other redundant variables were also removed before modelling.

Categorical variables were one-hot encoded, while numerical variables were standardized as part of the modelling pipeline.

Model

Linear Regression was used as the primary prediction model.

Because bike rental demand is positively skewed, the target variable was transformed using `log1p` during training. Predictions were converted back to the original scale for evaluation.

Evaluation

The model was evaluated using:

- R² Score
- Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
- Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
- Cross-validation
- Residual analysis
- Predicted vs actual visualization

Technologies

- Python
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Seaborn
- Plotly
- Scikit-learn
- Jupyter Notebook

